  "details": "The use of Euler angles makes it impossible to lock one axis and still allow for the full 360 degrees of rotation on that axis.\n\nThis is not gimbal lock; On both VRC and stock rotation constraints, Unity will prevent rotation past ±90 degrees when only one axis is allowed to rotate. This is because at this point, one or both of the other axes will want to set its value to flip by 180 degrees in the Euler angle representation. Naturally, it's kind of hard for Unity to actually do this when there's a rotation constraint forcing the values to remain unchanged.\n\nAs a consequence, it is currently impossible to make a free hinge using constraints that can rotate beyond ±90 degrees on its local axis.\n\nThis could be solved in two ways.\n\n1. A boolean to make constraints solve using quaternions instead of Euler angles. This would entirely bypass the problem. Personally, I don't like this, as it creates a \"legacy behavior\" setup where it's basically a checkbox to emulate the bugs of Unity native constraints.\n\n2. A dedicated \"hinge constraint\" which allows one local or world axis to rotate freely. This would be much more obvious to the user and feels a bit more intuitive to use, without imposing on existing behavior.",
